{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 336 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4b8790a45031a09e6ed39c214bbec59f9dfe13cde631b36a29bbd75b7097c5b9" :edb/db-before-t 335 :edb/db-after-t 336 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533648 50 #inst "2026-10-04T06:36:49.457Z" 13194139533648 true] [17592186103745 1000 #edb/ref 17592186244017 13194139533648 true] [17592186105624 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243978 13194139533648 true] [17592186105624 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243990 13194139533648 true] [17592186105624 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244000 13194139533648 true] [17592186105624 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244039 13194139533648 true] [17592186105624 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244046 13194139533648 true] [17592186105770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243981 13194139533648 true] [17592186105770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243984 13194139533648 true] [17592186105770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243987 13194139533648 true] [17592186105770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244025 13194139533648 true] [17592186105770 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244053 13194139533648 true] [17592186105775 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243974 13194139533648 true] [17592186105775 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243994 13194139533648 true] [17592186105775 1020 #edb/ref 17592186243997 13194139533648 true] [17592186105775 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244018 13194139533648 true] [17592186105778 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244004 13194139533648 true] [17592186105778 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244007 13194139533648 true] [17592186105778 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244011 13194139533648 true] [17592186105778 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244014 13194139533648 true] [17592186105778 1020 #edb/ref 17592186244032 13194139533648 true] [17592186106206 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533648 true] [17592186106206 1145 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4})=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243974 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243975 13194139533648 true] [17592186243974 1089 "q-300762" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243974 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533648 true] [17592186243974 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(13{x}^{13})=${{field-1}}" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243974 1113 #uuid "68755e41-b4eb-5dc8-908b-a53eae1bfe1a" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243974 1138 false 13194139533648 true] [17592186243974 1145 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }13{x}^{13} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(13\\cdot {x}^{13}) \\\\  & =13\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{13}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $13$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{13} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(13{x}^{13})=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243976 13194139533648 true] [17592186243975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243977 13194139533648 true] [17592186243975 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533648 true] [17592186243975 1041 "field-1" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243975 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243976 13194139533648 true] [17592186243975 1110 #uuid "5281085a-3c0e-5962-ac73-b1c25c1d2ae2" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243976 1034 "\\infty" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533648 true] [17592186243976 1111 #uuid "cd39d08a-28d3-552c-a980-5df424f6a7f4" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243977 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243977 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533648 true] [17592186243977 1111 #uuid "c15176a3-1b23-596e-b17c-02491bed22ef" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243978 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243979 13194139533648 true] [17592186243978 1089 "q-300766" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243978 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533648 true] [17592186243978 1100 "If $p(x)=10{x}^{8}$ and $q(x)=2{x}^{8},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533648 true] [17592186243978 1113 #uuid "a871e7e7-8cf4-59fa-8019-b14e04249778" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243978 1138 false 13194139533648 true] [17592186243978 1145 "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10{x}^{8})}{(2{x}^{8})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }5 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243979 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243980 13194139533648 true] [17592186243979 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533648 true] [17592186243979 1041 "field-1" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243979 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243980 13194139533648 true] [17592186243979 1110 #uuid "dc1cfda2-7434-581d-b5b1-6dd349750668" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243980 1034 "5" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533648 true] [17592186243980 1111 #uuid "852eda5a-fccf-5472-a04a-a7347400b59b" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243981 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243982 13194139533648 true] [17592186243981 1089 "q-301603" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243981 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533648 true] [17592186243981 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930904/assets/2fd351e75519ab242ba99028214cbdd35c61cea6acdf69a39eb30395e2f7eaaa.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5})=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533648 true] [17592186243981 1113 #uuid "5ffbc29c-ac41-5b4d-9058-2b01467fe05a" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243981 1138 false 13194139533648 true] [17592186243981 1145 "Applying the product rule and the root rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[5]{f(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-32.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{-32} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2{x}^{6} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{6}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-2$ causes $y=-2{x}^{6}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-2{x}^{6})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243983 13194139533648 true] [17592186243982 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533648 true] [17592186243982 1041 "field-1" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243982 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243983 13194139533648 true] [17592186243982 1110 #uuid "15a47ab1-576b-547c-8657-c38701392840" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243983 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533648 true] [17592186243983 1111 #uuid "e3b189bc-e353-56e7-b35e-490e5768f4f3" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243984 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243985 13194139533648 true] [17592186243984 1089 "q-301609" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243984 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533648 true] [17592186243984 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=${{field-1}}" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243984 1113 #uuid "501c8fcc-e277-5bee-85b9-ccc180b14c2c" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243984 1138 false 13194139533648 true] [17592186243984 1145 "First note that $ln⁡(0.9)<0.$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{4}$ looks like an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{4}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $ln⁡(0.9)$ causes $y=ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(ln⁡(0.9)\\cdot {x}^{4})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{4})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243986 13194139533648 true] [17592186243985 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533648 true] [17592186243985 1041 "field-1" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243985 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243986 13194139533648 true] [17592186243985 1110 #uuid "a5b72fd8-9b2e-5568-b3c9-50fa449c0230" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243986 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243986 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533648 true] [17592186243986 1111 #uuid "a3376395-9a94-5b75-9047-79428f921012" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243987 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243988 13194139533648 true] [17592186243987 1089 "q-301610" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243987 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533648 true] [17592186243987 1100 "If $p(x)=-20{x}^{6}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533648 true] [17592186243987 1113 #uuid "6c99095c-ef3c-5150-9fbc-24c606d52761" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243987 1138 false 13194139533648 true] [17592186243987 1145 "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-20{x}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }((-20\\cdot \\frac{1}{5})\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-4$ causes $y=-4{x}^{2}$ to be a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243988 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243989 13194139533648 true] [17592186243988 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533648 true] [17592186243988 1041 "field-1" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243988 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243989 13194139533648 true] [17592186243988 1110 #uuid "f613d2cb-38d1-5f1e-ac6e-57a420181f19" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243989 1034 "-\\infty" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243989 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533648 true] [17592186243989 1111 #uuid "e445182a-9d22-50fa-884c-cc6da48707f6" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243990 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243991 13194139533648 true] [17592186243990 1089 "q-302056" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243990 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533648 true] [17592186243990 1100 "If $p(x)=-5{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=9{x}^{6},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533648 true] [17592186243990 1113 #uuid "195543a3-3781-5c30-8836-03060a38637b" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243990 1138 false 13194139533648 true] [17592186243990 1145 "First, let's calculate ${x}^{4}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{4}\\cdot (-5{x}^{2})=-5{x}^{6}\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}}{(9{x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{5}{9}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243991 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243992 13194139533648 true] [17592186243991 1000 #edb/ref 17592186243993 13194139533648 true] [17592186243991 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533648 true] [17592186243991 1041 "field-1" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243991 1104 #edb/ref 17592186243993 13194139533648 true] [17592186243991 1110 #uuid "75bc54b5-f1d1-560b-859d-a9234d95610d" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243992 1034 "\\frac{5}{9}" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243992 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533648 true] [17592186243992 1111 #uuid "feeb959e-4ffb-5c20-badf-48dd9b4cb091" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243993 1034 "-\\frac{5}{9}" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243993 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533648 true] [17592186243993 1111 #uuid "195984d0-e524-5533-81eb-04e9a6d348c3" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243994 1017 #edb/ref 17592186243995 13194139533648 true] [17592186243994 1089 "q-336891" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243994 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533648 true] [17592186243994 1100 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13})=${{field-1}}" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243994 1113 #uuid "4f83014e-2628-5f35-ab54-6db1659f5989" 13194139533648 true] [17592186243994 1138 false 13194139533648 true] [17592186243994 1145 "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4\\cdot {x}^{13}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{13}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $4$ does not affect the limi